          "level": 2,
          "title": "Page 37",
          "body": "motivations. Some Callings can sit on either Side depending on\nhow the character expresses them. A character driven by justice\nmight be Heroic if they work within or alongside institutions, and\nUnaligned if they have concluded those institutions are broken\nbeyond repair and they are operating outside them entirely.\nThe Callings chapter lays this out clearly for each entry so there\nis no ambiguity at character creation.\nCHOOSING A SIDE\nThis decision should take about thirty seconds.\nThink about the character concept. Think about why this person\ndoes what they do and who they do it for.\n▪ If the answer is “for other people, because that is the right thing\nto do” — the character is Heroic.\n▪ If the answer is “for their own reasons, according to their own\ncode, and the fact that it sometimes helps people is secondary”\n— they are Unaligned.\nIf neither of those fits and the player is reaching for something\ndarker, that is a conversation to have with the GM about what\nkind of campaign this actually is.\nBOTTOM LINE\nMost heroes in HEROIC 5e are Heroic. Some are Unaligned. Both\nare valid. Both produce interesting stories. Both have full\nmechanical support throughout the game.\nPick the one that fits the character and move forward."
