          "id": "chapter-seven-talents-page-56",
          "level": 2,
          "title": "Page 56",
          "body": "CHAPTER SEVEN\nTALENTS\nTrained edges. Signature techniques. Hard-earned instincts. The heroic layer that makes\none hero play differently from another with the same class and powers.\n▪ Surveillance Specialist\nFIFTY TALENTS ▪ Takedown Artist\n▪ What Talents Do ▪ Team Player\n▪ When Talents Are Gained ▪ Tireless\n▪ Actor ▪ Tough\n▪ Alert ▪ Underwater Combatant\n▪ Appeal ▪ War Caster\n▪ Athlete ▪ Weapon Specialist\n▪ Battlefield Leader\nTalents are HEROIC 5e’s replacement for Feats. They are not\n▪ Cold Reader\nsimply Feats renamed. They are a distinct design layer: trained\n▪ Counterguard edges, signature techniques, hard-earned instincts, and\n▪ Defensive Fighter operational habits that make one hero play differently from\n▪ Durable another hero with the same class and powers.\n▪ Fast Draw\nHEROIC 5e separates character identity into clean layers. Origin\n▪ Field Commander\nexplains where the hero comes from. Class explains how the hero\n▪ Forensic Expert responds under pressure. Powers define what the hero can do\n▪ Grappler superhumanly. Skills define learned competency. Merits and\n▪ Hard to Kill Flaws define story-facing truths, complications, resources,\n▪ Improvised Fighter obligations, and social realities. Talents sit between Skills and\n▪ Inspiring Leader Class features as heroic edges that shape moment-to-moment\nplay.\n▪ Interrogator\n▪ Iron Will\nTHE SEPARATION THAT KEEPS EVERYTHING FAST\n▪ Jack of All Trades\nA Talent should never grant a superpower, replace a Skill, or\n▪ Linguist\nfunction as a disguised Merit. Flight is a Power. Fame is a Merit.\n▪ Lucky\nAcrobatics is a Skill. Acrobatic Combatant is a Talent. That\n▪ Martial Artist\nseparation keeps HEROIC 5e fast, readable, and modular.\n▪ Marksman\n▪ Master of Combat\nWHAT TALENTS DO\n▪ Master Negotiator\n▪ Mobile Talents answer one question: what has this hero learned to do\n▪ Never Give Up better, faster, cleaner, or under greater pressure than most\n▪ Observant people?\n▪ Pain Tolerance\nA Talent may represent training, instinct, experience, discipline,\n▪ Pattern Recognition\ncombat style, professional mastery, battlefield habit, or heroic\n▪ Performer nerve. It should feel useful at the table without becoming a new\n▪ Power Control subsystem. Talents provide one or two of the following:\n▪ Power Focus\n▪ A narrow but meaningful use of Advantage\n▪ Provocateur\n▪ A new Reaction or Bonus Action option\n▪ Psychic Discipline\n▪ A way to reduce friction in action scenes\n▪ Resilient\n▪ A specific combat or noncombat trick\n▪ Sharpshooter\n▪ A situational reroll\n▪ Skilled\n▪ A minor defensive edge\n▪ Specialist"
