        {
          "id": "chapter-seven-talents-page-61",
          "level": 2,
          "title": "Page 61",
          "body": "DESIGN NOTE\nBuilding the Skilled Hero, With or Without Powers\nNot every hero’s edge comes from a Power Set. Some heroes are\nsimply the most trained, most prepared, most knowledgeable\nperson in the room — the detective who reads a scene no one\nelse can, the tactician who has already planned for this, the\npolymath who always seems to have the one fact that matters.\nHEROIC 5e supports this hero without inventing a new subsystem\nfor it. Skills, Talents, and Expertise already do the job.\nSkilled and Specialist are both repeatable. Taken at every Talent\nopportunity (Levels 3 and 9, and later through Legacy Advance’s\nGrow option), they let a hero’s trained Skill list grow far past the\nfour picked at creation, with focused Advantage layered on top\nthrough Specialties. Jack of All Trades covers the gaps between\nthem, so nothing is ever a total blank. Expertise, earned through\nplay rather than purchased, is the ceiling — the moment the GM\nor an Origin recognizes that a hero’s dedication to a Skill has\nbecome genuine mastery.\nWith Powers. A Strategist with a Power Set (Super Genius, Armor\nSystems, or otherwise) uses Tactical Analysis and Tactical\nExploitation to convert that trained competence into battlefield\nadvantage, while the Power Set supplies the active combat\ntoolkit. This is the tactical genius who is also, mechanically, a\ngenuine threat in a fight — the inventor whose armor makes their\nintellect a weapon, the founder-scientist who built their own\narsenal from first principles.\nWithout Powers. A Trained-Origin hero who takes no Power Set\nat all still has a full build: Class Features from Strategist or any\nother Class, a growing wall of Skills and Specialties from\nrepeated Skilled and Specialist picks, and Talents doing the work\nPowers would otherwise do — Forensic Expert, Cold Reader,\nPattern Recognition, Interrogator, and the like. Their Power\nPicks, if any Origin grants them, can go toward Gear, Contacts, or\nother Merits rather than a Power Set. This is the hero who wins\nthe fight before it starts, because they were the only one who\nsaw it coming — a master field handler, a detective who has\nsimply never been wrong, someone whose entire arsenal is\npreparation.\nNeither version needs a new Power Set called “Super Skilled.”\nThat would spend Power Picks on what Skills, Talents, and\nExpertise already do — and would blur the line this game\ndeliberately draws between what a hero has learned and what a\nhero can do superhumanly."
        }
