          "title": "Page 62",
          "body": "CHAPTER EIGHT\nSKILLS\nSeventeen broad skills. What a hero has practiced, studied, trained in, and developed over\ntime.\nvigilante does not need separate skills for rooftop movement,\nTHIS CHAPTER grappling traversal, urban pursuit, evasive tumbling, and\n▪ How Skill Checks Work acrobatic recovery. Those all fall naturally under Acrobatics. A\n▪ Training & Expertise gadgeteer does not need separate skills for hacking, robotics,\nelectronics, cybernetics, and advanced systems engineering.\n▪ Skill Specialties\nThose all fall naturally under Technology.\n▪ Starting Skills\n▪ Gaining New Skills Broad Skills produce faster rulings, easier character creation,\n▪ Acrobatics cleaner gameplay, and more heroic competence without\nargument over edge cases. They keep attention on Powers,\n▪ Athletics\nTalents, battlefield action, and heroic identity rather than\n▪ Culture\nspreadsheet-style skill management.\n▪ Finesse\n▪ Influence\nHOW SKILL CHECKS WORK\n▪ Insight\n▪ Intimidation\nWhen a hero attempts something where training matters and the\n▪ Investigation\noutcome is uncertain, the GM calls for a Skill check.\n▪ Medicine\n▪ Notice 1d20 + Ability Score modifier + Prowess (if trained)\n▪ Occult\n▪ Science Training means the hero adds their Prowess to the roll. An\nuntrained hero rolls 1d20 + Ability Score modifier only.\n▪ Stealth\n▪ Streetwise Each Skill has a default Ability Score that governs its most\n▪ Survival common uses. The GM may authorize an alternate Ability Score\n▪ Technology pairing when the fiction supports it. Technology normally uses\nINT, but a desperate midair power armor repair under extreme\n▪ Vehicles\nphysical stress might use Technology with DEX. Athletics\n▪ Difficulty & Opposed Checks\nnormally uses STR, but endurance swimming might use CON.\n▪ Specialties Full Reference\nThese alternate pairings should reflect genuine fictional logic,\n▪ How It All Stacks\nnot optimization attempts.\nSkills represent learned competency. They are what a hero has\npracticed, studied, trained in, and developed over time. A hero TRAINING AND EXPERTISE\nwith high Athletics has put in the work. A hero with Medicine has\nstudied the body and emergency care. A hero with Streetwise A hero’s Skill competency has three levels, each building on the\nhas lived in the city long enough to know how it actually operates. last.\nSkills are distinct from Ability Scores, Powers, Talents, and\nLEVEL ONE\nMerits. An Ability Score describes what a person is. A Power\ndescribes what they can do superhumanly. A Talent describes a\nUNTRAINED\nsignature edge they have developed. A Skill describes what they\nhave learned. Roll 1d20 + Ability Score modifier. No Prowess added. A hero can\nstill attempt most tasks — they just lack the practiced edge that\nDESIGN NOTE training provides.\nWhy Broad Skills\nHEROIC 5e uses broad Skills deliberately. Superhero fiction does\nnot function like a medieval trade simulator. A rooftop-prowling"
