          "body": "Powers make the hero extraordinary. Skills make the hero\ncompetent. Talents make the hero sharp. All three matter and A NOTE ON SCALE\nnone of them substitutes for the others.\nThree Tiers, One Power Set\nThe Power Sets in Chapter 11 cover all three Campaign Ranks.\nPOWERS AND GEAR\nEvery Power Set has entries for all three Tiers. A Street Level\ncampaign uses only Tier 1 powers from each set. A Mid-Level\nWhen gear creates superhuman capability, build it using the\ncampaign uses up to Tier 2. A World Class campaign uses all\nPowers system. The gear is the narrative explanation for where\nthree Tiers including Apex powers.\nthe power comes from. Mechanically it is still a Power Set. This\nkeeps the game balanced and prevents mundane equipment This means a hero who begins play at Street Level and whose\nfrom replacing the Power Pick economy. campaign eventually elevates to World Class can continue\ndeveloping the same Power Set across the full ten levels. The\nTHE PRACTICAL DISTINCTION hero who began throwing small fireballs at Tier 1 is eventually\n▪ A grappling hook is gear. A web system that enables swinging, hurling citywide firestorms at Tier 3. That progression is the\nbinding, rapid rescue, and battlefield movement is a Power point.\nSet.\n▪ A motorcycle is gear. A transforming attack-cycle capable of\nimpossible pursuits, armored plating, and mounted weapon\nsystems is a Power Set and a Vehicle Merit.\n▪ If a piece of gear creates superhuman capability, build it as\nPowers. If it provides access, resources, or status, use the\nGear chapter or the Merits chapter.\nWhen gear is the source of a Power Set, the Gear Dependent\nLimitation and the Gear Dependent Flaw both apply at the\nappropriate Rating. If the gear is taken away, the hero loses the\nPower Set until it is recovered or rebuilt.\nREADING POWER SET ENTRIES\nEach Power Set entry in Chapter 11 follows a consistent format.\nEvery entry opens with a description of what the Power Set\nrepresents in superhero fiction and what kinds of heroes use it.\nThe following sections appear in every entry:\n▪ Suggested Attributes. Which Attributes most commonly\ngovern this Power Set and what each choice represents.\nSome Power Sets have a fixed Governing Ability. Most offer\ntwo or three options.\n▪ Associated Conditions. The conditions this Power Set most\ncommonly inflicts, exploits, or resists — indicating its tactical\nrole in combat.\n▪ Default Damage Track. For Power Sets with a repeated attack\npattern, the baseline formula for unarmed strikes or attacks\nfrom this Power Set.\n▪ Core Track. The four Core Track steps in order, each a passive\nbenefit defining scale and unlocking higher-Tier power\naccess.\n▪ Combat Powers. All At-Will, Encounter, Daily, and Reaction\npowers that affect combat.\n▪ Set-Specific Utility Powers. All noncombat, movement,\nsensory, survival, and support powers tied to this Power Set.\n▪ Enhancements. Common Enhancements appropriate to this\nPower Set with their specific effects.\n▪ Limitations. Common Limitations players often pair with this\nPower Set and what each restricts."
