          "level": 2,
          "title": "Page 198",
          "body": "CHAPTER SIXTEEN\nGM’S SECTION\nEverything on the other side of the table: how to run the game moment to moment, build\nenemies and encounters, run social scenes and investigations, use downtime, and sustain\na campaign over time.\nRunning HEROIC 5e is a different job from running most relevant Power, Talent, or Skill that makes the task trivially\nroleplaying games. The heroes are extraordinary. The stakes are achievable given who they are, say yes and move on.\ncity-scale or larger. The action is cinematic and fast. The\nRoll when tension serves the story. A Striker leaping between\nemotional temperature of every scene is higher than most games\ntwo rooftops does not need a roll unless something significant\naim for. A GM who tries to run HEROIC 5e like a careful fantasy\nhappens if they fail. A Striker leaping between rooftops while\ndungeon crawl will find it fights back. A GM who leans into what it\nbeing shot at with civilians below and a villain getting away: now\nis will find it one of the most rewarding games they have ever run.\nthe roll means something.\nThis section covers everything on the other side of the table. It\nassumes you have read the player-facing chapters. It does not THE GUIDE\nrepeat them — it builds on them from the perspective of the Ask yourself what happens if they fail before you call for the roll.\nperson who makes the world move. If the answer is nothing interesting, say yes.\nNever call for a roll as a tax on player agency. A hero who spends\nRUNNING HEROIC 5E\nan hour investigating a crime scene should find the clue. The roll\ndetermines how much they find and how clean the information is\nTHE PHILOSOPHY — not whether they find anything at all. Dead ends from dice\nrather than decisions are frustrating, not dramatic.\nHEROIC 5e is built around three words that appear throughout\nthis book. They are not aspirational. They are the design\nTHE GM AS REACTIVE ARCHITECT\nstandard against which every ruling, every scene call, and every\nmechanical decision should be measured. HEROIC 5e does not reward GMs who build elaborate plans and\nexecute them regardless of what players do. It rewards GMs who\nFast The table is always moving. No pause lasts longer than it\nbuild situations and then react to how players move through\nneeds to. When in doubt, make a call and move on. A slightly\nthem.\nwrong ruling that keeps the energy high beats a perfectly correct\nruling that kills momentum. Build a villain with a goal and a method. Build an environment\nwith features that matter. Build stakes that are clear and\nFun Everyone at the table is engaged, invested, and enjoying\npersonal. Then put the heroes in contact with all three and see\nthemselves — including you. If something is not working, change\nwhat happens. Your job in the session is not to execute your plan\nit. If a rule is producing bad outcomes, ignore it for this session\n— it is to react to theirs.\nand fix it later. The rules serve the game.\nPreparation should be in the form of tools, not plots. Know your\nDynamic The situation is always changing. The villain adapts.\nvillain’s personality and goals. Know the environment’s features\nConsequences of last session arrive in this one. Heroes who won\nand hazards. Know the stakes. Have a sense of what happens if\ndecisively face a world that has noticed. Heroes who caused\nthe heroes do nothing. Beyond that, react.\ncollateral damage face a world that remembers.\nMAKING FAILURE MOVE THE STORY\nWHEN TO ROLL AND WHEN TO SAY YES\nFailure in HEROIC 5e should never stop the story. It should\nThis is the most important skill in running HEROIC 5e and the one\nchange the direction. A failed Stealth check does not mean the\nthat separates sessions that feel exciting from sessions that feel\nheroes cannot infiltrate the building — it means they are detected\nlike paperwork.\nwhile doing it, which changes what kind of scene the infiltration\nRoll when the outcome is genuinely uncertain and the becomes. A failed Influence check does not mean the NPC is\nconsequences of failure are meaningfully different from success. permanently hostile — it means the approach that just failed is\nIf both paths lead to the same place, do not roll. If failure not working, which tells the heroes something true about the\nproduces nothing interesting, do not roll. If the hero has a situation."
